WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.360
<v Maya>Welcome to part three, the last part on the first cardio lecture.

2
00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:11.080
<v Maya>So far we have endurance, cross training, the three categories of aerobic activity, and blood circulation.

3
00:00:11.130 --> 00:00:12.730
<v Dev>And now the numbers.

4
00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:13.620
<v Maya>Yes.

5
00:00:13.670 --> 00:00:21.860
<v Maya>The key terms, the formulas, VO₂, workload with a worked case study, the long term benefits of cardio, and the questions from class.

6
00:00:21.910 --> 00:00:26.240
<v Maya>Take your time with the calculations, because we'll use them again in the second lecture.

7
00:00:26.290 --> 00:00:27.450
<v Maya>Start simple.

8
00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:31.380
<v Maya>Heart rate, HR, is the number of times the heart beats in a minute.

9
00:00:31.430 --> 00:00:35.960
<v Maya>Resting heart rate, RHR, is the same thing measured in a resting state.

10
00:00:36.010 --> 00:00:37.890
<v Dev>What counts as resting?

11
00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:39.970
<v Maya>Imagine waking up in the morning.

12
00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:41.880
<v Maya>You've made no physical effort.

13
00:00:41.930 --> 00:00:44.620
<v Maya>Only involuntary functions are going on.

14
00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:51.740
<v Maya>The heart beats, the brain works, the liver and kidneys work, body temperature and glucose are maintained, digestion carries on.

15
00:00:51.790 --> 00:00:57.710
<v Maya>The normal range of resting heart rate is sixty to eighty beats per minute, and the average is seventy two.

16
00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:01.220
<v Dev>So if someone says the average is sixty, they're wrong?

17
00:01:01.270 --> 00:01:08.520
<v Maya>That was a class answer, and the correction was that sixty to eighty is the range, and the average is a different number, seventy two.

18
00:01:08.570 --> 00:01:18.300
<v Maya>One thing to check, the E-book gives different values, sixty as good, forty as exceptionally good, and forty to a hundred as the general range, so check your course material.

19
00:01:18.350 --> 00:01:21.940
<v Maya>The lecture and slide use sixty to eighty and seventy two.

20
00:01:21.990 --> 00:01:24.240
<v Dev>Next, maximum heart rate.

21
00:01:24.290 --> 00:01:29.750
<v Maya>Maximum heart rate, MHR, is the maximum number of times the heart can beat in a minute.

22
00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:33.090
<v Maya>It's estimated as two hundred and twenty minus age.

23
00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:37.520
<v Maya>And heart rate reserve, HRR, is MHR minus RHR.

24
00:01:37.570 --> 00:01:40.780
<v Maya>We'll explain the logic behind that in lecture two.

25
00:01:40.830 --> 00:01:44.890
<v Dev>Okay, so reserve is the gap between resting and maximum.

26
00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:46.030
<v Maya>Exactly.

27
00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:48.070
<v Maya>Now stroke volume, SV.

28
00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:51.900
<v Maya>It's the amount of blood pumped by the left ventricle in one beat.

29
00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:53.510
<v Maya>Picture a spray bottle.

30
00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:56.640
<v Maya>One push of the pump, one stroke, sends out some liquid.

31
00:01:56.690 --> 00:01:58.830
<v Maya>That amount is the stroke volume.

32
00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:01.070
<v Maya>The bottle is the left ventricle.

33
00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:05.130
<v Maya>The average resting stroke volume is seventy millilitres per beat.

34
00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:07.070
<v Dev>And watch the unit, right?

35
00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:07.960
<v Maya>Yes.

36
00:02:08.010 --> 00:02:11.410
<v Maya>Exam options may say per beat, per second or per minute.

37
00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:13.320
<v Maya>Stroke volume is per beat.

38
00:02:13.370 --> 00:02:20.130
<v Maya>A small note, the lecture and slides say left ventricle, and the E-book says each ventricle, so check your course material.

39
00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:22.070
<v Dev>Then cardiac output.

40
00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:27.100
<v Maya>Cardiac output, Q, is the amount of blood pumped by the left ventricle in one minute.

41
00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:30.790
<v Maya>The formula is Q equals stroke volume times heart rate.

42
00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:33.400
<v Maya>It isn't a special formula, it's logic.

43
00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:36.980
<v Maya>Blood per beat, times beats per minute, gives blood per minute.

44
00:02:37.030 --> 00:02:38.660
<v Dev>Do the rest value.

45
00:02:38.710 --> 00:02:42.900
<v Maya>At rest, stroke volume is seventy, resting heart rate is seventy two.

46
00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:50.240
<v Maya>Seventy times seventy two is five thousand and forty millilitres, close to five thousand, so about five litres per minute.

47
00:02:50.290 --> 00:02:51.960
<v Dev>And during cardio?

48
00:02:52.010 --> 00:02:55.690
<v Maya>That's the acute response, the immediate effect of doing cardio.

49
00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:58.980
<v Maya>Heart rate increases, and cardiac output increases.

50
00:02:59.030 --> 00:03:03.570
<v Maya>One student said Q stays the same, but it can't, because there's physical effort now.

51
00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:10.770
<v Maya>The lecture says the resting five litres is speeded up five times, so five times five is twenty five litres per minute.

52
00:03:10.820 --> 00:03:16.210
<v Maya>The slide says a sportsman reaches thirty to thirty two litres during maximal exercise.

53
00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:23.370
<v Maya>Those are different cases, an average person versus a sportsman at maximal effort, so check which one your course wants.

54
00:03:23.420 --> 00:03:24.770
<v Dev>Quick recap?

55
00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:26.680
<v Maya>Heart rate, beats per minute.

56
00:03:26.730 --> 00:03:29.890
<v Maya>Resting rate, sixty to eighty, average seventy two.

57
00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:32.270
<v Maya>Maximum, two twenty minus age.

58
00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:34.660
<v Maya>Reserve, maximum minus resting.

59
00:03:34.710 --> 00:03:37.680
<v Maya>Stroke volume, about seventy millilitres per beat.

60
00:03:37.730 --> 00:03:44.240
<v Maya>Cardiac output, stroke volume times heart rate, about five litres at rest, about twenty five in the acute response.

61
00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:45.550
<v Maya>Now VO₂.

62
00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:50.150
<v Maya>The V stands for volume, the actual volume of oxygen, not the ventricle.

63
00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:51.820
<v Maya>O2 is oxygen.

64
00:03:51.870 --> 00:03:57.890
<v Maya>So VO₂ is the volume of oxygen the body consumes to produce energy, ATP.

65
00:03:57.940 --> 00:03:59.750
<v Dev>And VO₂ rest?

66
00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:01.330
<v Maya>The same thing at rest.

67
00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:06.350
<v Maya>Even sitting still, your involuntary functions need energy, so they need some oxygen.

68
00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:08.120
<v Maya>It depends on body weight.

69
00:04:08.170 --> 00:04:14.260
<v Maya>VO₂ rest is three point five millilitres of oxygen per kilogram of body weight per minute.

70
00:04:14.310 --> 00:04:17.190
<v Maya>This value is also called one MET.

71
00:04:17.240 --> 00:04:18.700
<v Dev>Do an example.

72
00:04:18.750 --> 00:04:21.000
<v Maya>A person weighing eighty kilograms.

73
00:04:21.050 --> 00:04:27.550
<v Maya>Three point five times eighty is two hundred and eighty millilitres of oxygen per minute, just to stay alive.

74
00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:33.260
<v Maya>Quick note, the board photo can look like thirty eight, but the slide and E-book say three point five.

75
00:04:33.310 --> 00:04:35.380
<v Dev>What does MET mean?

76
00:04:35.430 --> 00:04:37.540
<v Maya>Metabolic equivalent of task.

77
00:04:37.590 --> 00:04:41.650
<v Maya>Think of it as an energy currency, the amount you spend to buy oxygen.

78
00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:43.630
<v Maya>At rest it's one MET.

79
00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:46.400
<v Maya>As activity rises, the number rises.

80
00:04:46.450 --> 00:04:52.140
<v Maya>The lecture gave illustrations, three or four for jogging and running, and nine or ten for a sprint.

81
00:04:52.190 --> 00:04:55.480
<v Maya>These were only illustrations, not slide values.

82
00:04:55.530 --> 00:04:57.350
<v Dev>And VO₂ max?

83
00:04:57.400 --> 00:05:02.490
<v Maya>Imagine a graph, heart rate on the vertical axis, oxygen volume on the horizontal.

84
00:05:02.540 --> 00:05:04.540
<v Maya>A brisk walk sits low on both.

85
00:05:04.590 --> 00:05:05.930
<v Maya>A jog is higher.

86
00:05:05.980 --> 00:05:07.480
<v Maya>A run is higher still.

87
00:05:07.530 --> 00:05:14.450
<v Maya>A sprint takes someone close to their maximum heart rate, and the oxygen consumed at that maximum is VO₂ max.

88
00:05:14.500 --> 00:05:19.050
<v Maya>It's the person's aerobic capacity, their maximum aerobic capacity.

89
00:05:19.100 --> 00:05:22.340
<v Maya>How it's tested comes in the fitness testing lectures.

90
00:05:22.390 --> 00:05:23.910
<v Dev>Workload next.

91
00:05:23.960 --> 00:05:26.290
<v Maya>Workload is the amount of work done.

92
00:05:26.340 --> 00:05:31.960
<v Maya>In weight training four factors tell it, range of motion, poundage, speed and number of reps.

93
00:05:32.010 --> 00:05:36.290
<v Maya>In cardio, three factors, distance covered, speed and time taken.

94
00:05:36.340 --> 00:05:39.630
<v Maya>Changing just one is enough to raise the workload.

95
00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:41.030
<v Dev>Examples.

96
00:05:41.080 --> 00:05:45.340
<v Maya>Distance, a person who jogs two kilometres is told to do two and a half.

97
00:05:45.390 --> 00:05:49.270
<v Maya>Speed, treadmill from seven to seven and a half kilometres per hour.

98
00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:54.980
<v Maya>Time, a person used to ten minutes of cycling adds ten minutes of running and ten minutes of swimming.

99
00:05:55.030 --> 00:05:58.160
<v Maya>That raises time and also uses cross training.

100
00:05:58.210 --> 00:05:59.710
<v Dev>And the case study?

101
00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:01.740
<v Maya>Goal, don't change the distance.

102
00:06:01.790 --> 00:06:03.880
<v Maya>The client covers two kilometres.

103
00:06:03.930 --> 00:06:05.810
<v Maya>Old time was fifteen minutes.

104
00:06:05.860 --> 00:06:07.900
<v Maya>New time must be twelve minutes.

105
00:06:07.950 --> 00:06:11.990
<v Maya>The workload has gone up, because the same distance in less time is harder.

106
00:06:12.040 --> 00:06:13.980
<v Maya>So speed has to increase.

107
00:06:14.030 --> 00:06:17.240
<v Dev>Speed equals distance divided by time.

108
00:06:17.290 --> 00:06:23.080
<v Maya>Yes, and the unit is kilometres per hour, so minutes must become hours, by dividing by sixty.

109
00:06:23.130 --> 00:06:27.060
<v Maya>Old, fifteen minutes is a quarter of an hour, zero point two five.

110
00:06:27.110 --> 00:06:31.410
<v Maya>Two divided by zero point two five is eight kilometres per hour.

111
00:06:31.460 --> 00:06:35.660
<v Maya>New, twelve minutes divided by sixty is zero point two hours.

112
00:06:35.710 --> 00:06:39.480
<v Maya>Two divided by zero point two is ten kilometres per hour.

113
00:06:39.530 --> 00:06:41.810
<v Maya>So speed rose from eight to ten.

114
00:06:41.860 --> 00:06:43.920
<v Dev>All three factors together.

115
00:06:43.970 --> 00:06:44.800
<v Maya>Exactly.

116
00:06:44.850 --> 00:06:48.630
<v Maya>Chronic means long term and adaptations means benefits.

117
00:06:48.680 --> 00:06:51.230
<v Maya>Cardiovascular adaptations first.

118
00:06:51.280 --> 00:06:56.080
<v Maya>Better contractility of the myocardium, the heart muscle, meaning better pumping.

119
00:06:56.130 --> 00:07:00.810
<v Maya>That leads to increased stroke volume and increased exercise cardiac output.

120
00:07:00.860 --> 00:07:02.810
<v Maya>Decreased resting heart rate.

121
00:07:02.860 --> 00:07:07.910
<v Maya>Increased red blood cell count, the transport agents carrying oxygen and nutrients.

122
00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:10.780
<v Maya>Neocapillarization, more new capillaries.

123
00:07:10.830 --> 00:07:16.070
<v Maya>Increased elasticity of blood vessels, so they regain their shape and blood flows smoothly.

124
00:07:16.120 --> 00:07:20.000
<v Maya>Increased mitochondrial density, more energy production per cell.

125
00:07:20.050 --> 00:07:24.760
<v Maya>Then increased resistance to fatigue, you tire later, and increased endurance.

126
00:07:24.810 --> 00:07:26.800
<v Dev>Is there a way to group them?

127
00:07:26.850 --> 00:07:27.690
<v Maya>Yes.

128
00:07:27.740 --> 00:07:32.540
<v Maya>More capillaries, more red blood cells and more mitochondria are about quantity.

129
00:07:32.590 --> 00:07:35.950
<v Maya>Tiring later and higher endurance are about quality.

130
00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:37.550
<v Dev>And respiratory?

131
00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:42.260
<v Maya>Respiratory muscles get stronger, the diaphragm and intercostal muscles.

132
00:07:42.310 --> 00:07:44.840
<v Maya>Lung volume and capacity increase.

133
00:07:44.890 --> 00:07:47.640
<v Maya>Blood flow to the lobes of the lungs improves.

134
00:07:47.690 --> 00:07:50.210
<v Maya>The alveoli are used more efficiently.

135
00:07:50.260 --> 00:07:52.210
<v Maya>Gaseous exchange increases.

136
00:07:52.260 --> 00:07:56.800
<v Maya>And recovery from exercise improves, so you get back to normal faster.

137
00:07:56.850 --> 00:07:58.730
<v Dev>Now the class questions.

138
00:07:58.780 --> 00:08:03.070
<v Maya>First, some people say cardio isn't necessary, only strength training.

139
00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:06.900
<v Maya>The answer is that all components of fitness are interdependent.

140
00:08:06.950 --> 00:08:10.040
<v Maya>You don't leave your heart and lungs at home on a strength day.

141
00:08:10.090 --> 00:08:17.460
<v Maya>Oxygenated blood is needed, and that's partly why rest intervals between sets matter, for cardiorespiratory recovery.

142
00:08:17.510 --> 00:08:22.080
<v Maya>How much cardio depends on the goal, for a marathon more, for bodybuilding less.

143
00:08:22.130 --> 00:08:25.440
<v Maya>But in general cardio is important for everyone.

144
00:08:25.490 --> 00:08:28.030
<v Dev>And cardio first or weights first?

145
00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:31.300
<v Maya>That's a scheduling question, answered in the scheduling lectures.

146
00:08:31.350 --> 00:08:32.510
<v Maya>Pause and think.

147
00:08:32.560 --> 00:08:34.800
<v Maya>A person weighs sixty kilograms.

148
00:08:34.850 --> 00:08:37.730
<v Maya>How much oxygen per minute do they need at rest?

149
00:08:37.780 --> 00:08:39.890
<v Dev>Three point five times sixty.

150
00:08:39.940 --> 00:08:42.820
<v Dev>That's two hundred and ten millilitres per minute.

151
00:08:42.870 --> 00:08:43.810
<v Maya>Correct.

152
00:08:43.860 --> 00:08:46.930
<v Maya>And two kilometres in ten minutes, what's the speed?

153
00:08:46.980 --> 00:08:53.480
<v Dev>Ten minutes is one sixth of an hour, so two divided by one sixth is twelve kilometres per hour.

154
00:08:53.530 --> 00:08:54.380
<v Maya>Right.

155
00:08:54.430 --> 00:08:56.440
<v Maya>Here are the five things to remember.

156
00:08:56.490 --> 00:09:02.450
<v Maya>One, resting heart rate sixty to eighty, average seventy two, maximum is two twenty minus age.

157
00:09:02.500 --> 00:09:10.890
<v Maya>Two, stroke volume is about seventy millilitres per beat, and cardiac output is stroke volume times heart rate, about five litres at rest.

158
00:09:10.940 --> 00:09:16.660
<v Maya>Three, VO₂ rest is three point five millilitres per kilogram per minute, one MET.

159
00:09:16.710 --> 00:09:22.000
<v Maya>Four, cardio workload is distance, speed and time, and speed is distance over time.

160
00:09:22.050 --> 00:09:27.910
<v Maya>Five, chronic adaptations improve both quantity and quality, in the heart, vessels and lungs.

161
00:09:27.960 --> 00:09:30.230
<v Dev>Next lecture, intensity.

162
00:09:30.280 --> 00:09:31.520
<v Maya>See you in part four.

